{"created": "2026-07-27T06:52:44.276410+00:00", "modified": "2026-07-27T06:52:44.493533+00:00", "id": 21619015, "conceptrecid": "21619014", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.21619015", "conceptdoi": "10.5281/zenodo.21619014", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21619015", "metadata": {"title": "IEA Wind Task 52 WG1: A proposal for new metrics to evaluate lidar-measured TI (White Paper)", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.21619015", "publication_date": "2026-07-27", "description": "<p>This white paper presents a new set of performance metrics for assessing the ability of vertical profiling lidar&nbsp;systems to measure turbulence intensity (TI) for wind turbine load assessment applications. In contrast&nbsp;to existing approaches, which typically rely on correlations of 10-minute averaged TI values, the&nbsp;proposed methodology applies load-relevant metrics &mdash; namely effective turbulence intensity, damage&nbsp;index, and representative TI based on the 90% quantile&mdash;directly when comparing lidar-derived TI to&nbsp;collocated reference measurements from cup anemometers. By evaluating the same derived quantities&nbsp;that are used in wind turbine load calculations, the approach removes the ambiguity inherent in&nbsp;correlation-based assessments and provides a clearer link between measurement performance and&nbsp;downstream application relevance. In addition, the paper proposes a testing framework that supports&nbsp;the incremental compilation of a database of TI assessment results over time. Rather than requiring<br>a single, exhaustive test campaign, this framework enables new products &mdash; such as TI correction&nbsp;methodologies or updated reconstruction algorithms &mdash; to be introduced with an initially limited&nbsp;scope of validated conditions that can be systematically expanded as further evidence becomes available.<br>This pragmatic approach acknowledges the uncontrollable nature of environmental conditions&nbsp;during free-field testing while promoting transparency, comparability, and confidence in the use of&nbsp;lidar-derived TI for wind turbine load assessments. The paper reflects the consensus findings of an&nbsp;IEA Task 52 working group, based on collective discussions and complementary investigations carried&nbsp;out by individual members.</p>", "access_right": "open", "creators": [{"name": "Brandetti, L.", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Cavalli, M.", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Chattopadhyay, S.", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Cressall, S.", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Eichelmann, T.", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Fruehmann, R. K.", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Haghshenas, A.", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Hansen, A.", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Macdonald, E.", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Parker, Z.", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Rabhi, H.", "affiliation": null}, {"name": "Steudel, D.", "affiliation": null}], "resource_type": {"title": "Project deliverable", "type": "publication", "subtype": "deliverable"}, "license": {"id": "cc-by-4.0"}, "communities": [{"id": "ieawindtask52"}], "relations": {"version": [{"index": 0, "is_last": true, "parent": {"pid_type": "recid", "pid_value": "21619014"}}]}}, "title": "IEA Wind Task 52 WG1: A proposal for new metrics to evaluate lidar-measured TI (White Paper)", "links": {"self": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/21619015", "self_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/21619015", "preview_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/21619015?preview=1", "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21619015", "self_doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21619015", "self_doi_html": "https://zenodo.org/doi/10.5281/zenodo.21619015", "reserve_doi": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/21619015/draft/pids/doi", "parent": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/21619014", "parent_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/21619014", "parent_doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21619014", "parent_doi_html": "https://zenodo.org/doi/10.5281/zenodo.21619014", "self_iiif_manifest": "https://zenodo.